  "_nonstandard_note": "nonstandard: what people say that writing doesn't want: almost, the standard form shown, one way only (good for well and a flat adverb, walk slow for walk slowly, never the other way). A double negative only after a negative in its sentence, so Is there nothing to eat? stays wrong for anything. Not a worksheet item's spoken, which is right: COURSE.md, Spoken forms. English A2's parts a and c add what grown-ups really say: were for was and was for were (It were raining, We was late: the North's, Cumbria's), seen for saw and done for did (I seen it), sung, rung, drunk, swum, begun for sang, rang, drank, swam, began, a past for a participle after have, has, had (I've saw, I've went, she's took: Scotland's, Ulster's, the North's), not never for never (I haven't never been). Not here, and wrong: a child's or a learner's regular past (telled, catched, knowed, throwed, growed), as gooder and more bigger; I've knew and I've flew, too doubtful to keep. Come, give and run for came, gave and ran are said too, but stay wrong: they are the cue copied, and the past is what the unit teaches (on the native list). English B1 adds, for the British English the course teaches: been for have been (I been waiting), used after did and use before to, ain't, innit and isn't it for any person; a past for a participle after be (It was broke, She was took), what after a noun (the man what lives there), hisself, meself, theirselves; had have for had (If I'd have known), of for have after a modal (would of), a question's order kept in a reported question (He asked did I want tea). Not here, and wrong: would after if (If I would have a boat, If it would rain), the classic mistake from German and Slovene, not what this variety says. English B2, part b, adds: who for whom after a small word (to who I spoke), what for which with words before it (The van broke down, what was a pity), stood and sat for standing and sitting after am, is, are or a noun (the man stood by the gate, I'm sat here: the North's; part a has them after was, were, been), neither for either and none for any after a negative, neither … or for nor, the both of them. Not here, and wrong: that after a comma (no source ties it to this variety).",
